{"id":"flame_torch","compound_name":"Simulation::Aero::flame_torch","source_path":"flame_torch/flame_torch.json","category":"Fire","author":"Autodesk","description":"This graph uses Aero to produce a torch-style flame","documentation_file":"flame_torch_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Flame Torch Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses aero with combustion to create a flame torch simulation. It's a great reference for how fire is created using Bifrost. It's a perfect starting point for camp fires, and most other effects requiring setting an object on fire.\n\n### Inputs and outputs\n\nInputs for this graph are the meshes you wish to set fire to.  The output is the resulting aero volume from the fire simulation.  \n\n### Useful information\n\n- Performance is directly related to the `fluid detail size` on the `source_air` node, and the `style` of the flames.  Highly wrinkled flames which use fluffy, busy or wispy `style` on the solver settings, along with vorticity or turbulence influences will require a smaller detail size and take longer to compute compared to `smooth` flames which can still look good with larger detail sizes and use smooth style and low vorticity.  \n\n- The speed of movement for the flames is directly related to scene scale. Smaller objects burn with very spastic and rapidly changing flames, whereas bigger objects burn with slower, smoother moving flames.  Please adjust the `scene units in meters` on the `aero_solver_settings` appropriately, or scale your geometry.  \n\n### Example scene\n\nThe example scenes provides an example of how this effect works great for both moving and stationary geometry.  It also uses geometry which is actually to scale, rather than using the Scene Units in Meters property on the solver.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"display_geo","portDirection":"output","portType":"a