fuel source for the combustion solver. \nThe **burn_rate** property is set lower to get a \"tamer\" flame.  If you want a hotter, more energetic flame, increase this property."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Combustion results can vary significantly at different resolutions. While it's helpful to dial in, don't expect 1:1 results."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Scene Scale\nFor any simulated system **scene scale** is super important.\n\nThe scene scale in a Aero graph is found in the solver settings and is called *scene_units_in_meters*.\n\n"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***post_refine_aero***\n\nThis compound adds high resolution detail to enhance the look of aero and combustion simulations.\n\nThis workflow will yield more consistent results as opposed to just cranking the fluid resolution if you need more resolution in the fire.\n\nIn this graph, this is off by default, feel free to turn it back on but be aware that it will slow down the execution of the graph."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The *modulate_influence* uses a simulation property to scale the amount of effect applied by other influences. "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Each component of the turbulence is animated at different rates.  The *time* output (seconds) is multiplied by different values."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Adjust y-turbulence\n\nThe look of the fire demands limiting the y-turbulence.  This compound offers the ability to limit the y-direction to up only (***absolute_value***) and scaling that y-value (*multiply_y_value*)"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# Adjust y-turbulence\n\nThe look of the fire demands limiting the y-turbulence.  This compound offers the ability to limit the y-direction to up only (***absolute_value***) and scaling that y-value (*multiply_y_value*)"}]}